    "q-81200": {
      "dom_id": "step-q81200",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Given the vector $x$ and the system of linear equations below, find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system.\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}-8{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we need to find a basis of the solution space of the system. \n\nConsider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system, which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -5 & -8 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -8 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are $2$ pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. Now, from the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=2{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-3\\cdot (2{x}_{3})=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=6{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}6{x}_{3} \\\\ 2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is a basis of the solution space of the system.\n\nFinally, we find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot b}{b\\cdot b}b \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\cdot 6+5\\cdot 2+0\\cdot 1}{{6}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}b \\\\  & =\\frac{82}{41}b \\\\  & =2b \\\\  & =2[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-81200-a",
